[
  {
    "id": "recordings",
    "invitation": "What is something you’d like to change, but can’t change on your own?",
    "contributions": [
      {
        "id": "e-a1",
        "text": "I want to save the village recordings before the tapes fall apart. There are songs and interviews that nobody has copied. I can lend a tape player, but we need equipment and people to do the work. I would love anyone to be able to hear them."
      },
      {
        "id": "e-a2",
        "text": "I worry about putting old recordings online. My aunt spoke at a wedding as if she was talking to her family, not to a searchable public archive. I want families to have a say before voices like hers are made public."
      },
      {
        "id": "e-a3",
        "text": "I would like captions on the local recordings. I cannot hear much of the speech. But a transcript that leaves out the laughter or a long pause can miss why something matters. I could help check captions if someone else makes a first version."
      }
    ],
    "composition": "We want to save these voices before the tapes fall apart. A borrowed player is a start; equipment and people could keep the songs and interviews from disappearing. It is tempting to think that saving them means letting everybody hear.\n\nBut a voice kept safe is not necessarily a voice made public. Words spoken at a family wedding were offered to a particular room. Can we preserve that difference when a recording becomes searchable? Families need a say in what happens to those words.\n\nAnd being allowed to listen is not yet being able to hear. Captions could open the recordings to more of us, though a pause or a burst of laughter can carry what a tidy transcript leaves out.\n\nSo keeping the voices asks more of us than copying the tapes. We need to attend to whom they were speaking, who can receive them now, and what disappears in between."
  },
  {
    "id": "orchestra",
    "invitation": "What is something you’d like to change, but can’t change on your own?",
    "contributions": [
      {
        "id": "e-b1",
        "text": "I wish our amateur orchestra could play more difficult music. We keep returning to pieces we already know. I could lead an extra rehearsal, but I need enough other players to want the challenge too."
      },
      {
        "id": "e-b2",
        "text": "I only started the clarinet last year. I love the orchestra, but I cannot keep up when the tempo increases. I would like a rehearsal where stopping is allowed and I can try the difficult bit again without feeling that I am wasting everybody’s evening."
      },
      {
        "id": "e-b3",
        "text": "We spend every orchestra rehearsal preparing for the concert. I would like an evening where we play for pleasure and nobody is judging whether it is ready for an audience. I still like concerts. I just want some music that does not have to become one."
      }
    ],
    "composition": "We want the difficult passage: the music we cannot quite play yet, the reason to come back next week. We want to stop in the middle of it, too, and try again without feeling that our learning has cost everybody else their evening.\n\nThere is pleasure in reaching further, and pleasure that disappears when every note becomes preparation for an audience. An extra rehearsal might give the ambitious music more space. It does not by itself make stopping easier, or give us an evening that belongs to playing rather than getting ready.\n\nCan difficulty be something we enter at different speeds? We could want the concert and still want music that never has to become one. We could make room for the player ready to lead another rehearsal and the player still finding the notes. The question is what we would need to change together for both to want to return."
  }
]

